Numbers to inspect before accepting the instrument:
- The achieved cohort rate should track 717000/s within 1%; all measurement
  sends must have completed (a disconnect/error/100 s drain timeout fails).
- Inspect stderr pacing lag, maximum worker send gap, omitted arrivals and
  queued bytes. A gap over 2 ms, substantial lag/catch-up, or write backlog
  invalidates a claim that the observed tail comes solely from the server.
- Read outstanding_max and the global any-connection over-64 time fraction
  with the end-of-window pending count and drain duration. The 64 threshold
  does not limit sending. Queue accumulation is the saturation witness.
- Report short and long count/mean/p50/p90/p99/p99.9/p99.99/max and combined
  mean/percentiles. The short p99.9 JSON field p999_ms is the tail-cell score.
  Compare same-binary repeats before interpreting differences between server
  candidates; the maintainer owns the repeatability and reorder verdict.

Window semantics: JSON rate is replied measurement-send count / 20 seconds.
Histograms exclude warmup sends and include replies drained after window end.
Stderr also reports wall-window reply throughput (including warmup backlog),
drain time, and generator pressure. Preserve both streams with each result.
See tools/tailgen/README.md for precise saturation and histogram semantics.

